<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What's in Your Bag?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Share simple stories from books or personal creations. Interactive: Interact with questions and answers about a classroom topic.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can share simple stories about object locations (e.g., "Maria is looking for the book. Where's the book? Oh, it's on the desk."). Can ask and answer questions about people in the classroom (e.g., "What is the teacher's name?" "The teacher's name is Miss Maria."). Can describe classroom objects using basic words and phrases (e.g., "I have a red pencil.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "What's in the Bag?" circle game. Students sit in a circle. The teacher holds a bag (their own or a mystery bag with classroom objects). The teacher says, "What's in my bag?" and pulls out an object (e.g., a "red pencil"). Students repeat, "A red pencil!" and then pass the bag around. Each student pulls out an object, says its name, and a simple description (e.g., "A blue crayon," "A small eraser"). Students use vocabulary: pencil, crayon, eraser, book, ruler, notebook, pen, marker. This activates prior knowledge and simple speaking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models asking and answering using a full sentence with a preposition. Teacher points to their desk and says, "What's on my desk? A book. The book is on my desk." Then points to their bag and says, "What's in my bag? A pencil case. The pencil case is in my bag." Teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses realia and gestures to check understanding of prepositions and objects.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a book above a desk) "Is the book on the desk?" (Expected: "No!") Then places it on the desk: "Now, is the book on the desk?" (Expected: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a crayon and a pencil) "Is this a pen?" (Expected: "No!") "What is it?" (Expected: "It's a crayon! It's a pencil!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is the eraser?" (Expected: "It's in the pencil case!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture of a "messy" and an "organized" classroom desk, featuring various classroom objects (book, pencil, ruler, marker, notebook, crayon, eraser, paper) with some placed in, on, next to, behind, or between other objects. A short, illustrated dialogue about two friends, Maria and Carlos, describing their desks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand short, simple dialogues about familiar classroom objects and their locations, and identify key information regarding object descriptions and positions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br/>
                    Maria: "Look at my desk! It's neat. My book is on the desk. My red pencil is in my pencil case."<br/>
                    Carlos: "Wow! My desk is messy. Where is my ruler? Oh, it's behind the notebook. And my blue crayon is under the paper!"<br/>
                    Maria: "What's in your bag, Carlos?"<br/>
                    Carlos: "In my bag, I have a large map and a green marker."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (Teacher asks, students point/say a word):</b> "Who has a neat desk?" (Maria) "Who has a messy desk?" (Carlos) "What color is Maria's pencil?" (Red)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Students say "True" or "False"):</b> "Maria's book is under the desk." (False) "Carlos has a map in his bag." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Teacher says sentence, students point to picture):</b> "The ruler is behind the notebook." (Students point to Carlos's desk picture).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using WH-questions (What's, Where's), Present Simple ("is," "have"), and Prepositions of Place (in, on, next to, between, behind) with target vocabulary (desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map; neat, messy, organized, large, small, colorful, light blue, green).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice - "Sentence Chain":</b> The teacher starts a sentence about a classroom object, and students complete it using a target preposition. Teacher: "The marker is..." Student 1: "...on the desk." Teacher: "The pencil is..." Student 2: "...in the pencil case." Continue around the room. Use sentence frames on the board for support: "The ___ is ___ the ___." Focus on /ɔ/ /-n/ in "on" and /ˈp-eɪp-ər/ in "paper."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice - "Find and Describe":</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a classroom object (e.g., "book"). Students, in pairs, orally ask each other, "Where is the book?" and then provide an answer based on what they see in the classroom or from a picture. Student A: "Where is the book?" Student B: "The book is on the shelf." (or "It's on my desk."). Encourage using descriptive adjectives like "red book" or "large board." Teacher circulates to provide pronunciation feedback and ensure correct grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction - "Classroom Scavenger Hunt (Oral)":</b> In pairs, students receive a checklist of 5 classroom objects. They must find them and ask/answer questions about their locations. Example: Student A: "Where is the eraser?" Student B: "The eraser is next to the crayon." Then they switch roles for the next item. The goal is accurate spoken sentences using prepositions and WH-questions. Emphasize "Excuse me, where is the..." for sociolinguistic practice.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Partner Information Gap Activity - "My Mystery Desk". Students work in pairs. Each student receives a picture of a classroom desk with various objects, but each picture has slightly different object arrangements or different objects. Students cannot look at their partner's picture. They must describe their own picture and ask questions to discover the differences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students sit facing each other, with a barrier (e.g., a book or folder) between them so they cannot see their partner's picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Student A starts by describing an object on their desk: "On my desk, I have a red book. It's next to a small blue crayon."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Student B listens and then asks a question to clarify or find a difference: "Do you have a blue crayon?" or "Where is your ruler?" or "What color is your pencil?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Students take turns describing and asking questions until they find at least three differences or have described all key objects. Encourage them to confirm locations: "Is it here?" "Yes, it's here." or "No, it's on the shelf."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. The teacher circulates, facilitating communication, prompting with "What's in your bag?" or "Where is the...?" and providing support for fluency rather than strict accuracy. The focus is on active spoken interaction and task completion.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "My Mystery Desk" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "My Mystery Desk" information gap activity in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking Focus):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clearly pronounces target words like "book" (/b/), "table" (/t/), and common prepositions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 5-7 classroom object nouns, adjectives (red, blue, small), and verbs (have, is, find).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Correctly uses "What's in/on...?" and "It's in/on/next to..." for object location.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Communicates ideas understandably, with minimal hesitation, allowing the partner to follow.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in asking and answering questions with their partner.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-assessment by circling "Yes" or "No" on a small paper slip:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask, "What's in your bag?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can tell my partner where an object is (e.g., "It's on the desk.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can describe an object with a color (e.g., "a red pencil").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did you use today when talking to your friend?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to say? What was a little difficult?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "My Mystery Desk" activity effectively engaged students in speaking. Most students were able to ask basic WH-questions and use "is" and "on/in" with acceptable pronunciation. Some struggled with "next to" and "between." Phonemic awareness for /b/ and /t/ was practiced, but more targeted blending practice for multi-syllable words like "paper" or "marker" could be beneficial. Adjustments for the next lesson could include providing more visual cues for trickier prepositions and dedicated short drills for specific sounds/blends. Students demonstrated respectful classroom interactions, asking "Excuse me" at times, which is a good start.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to orally describe objects and their locations. In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will transfer these spoken descriptions into written sentences, drawing on the vocabulary, grammar, and prepositions practiced today to create simple written descriptions of their own classroom items or fictional "bags."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>